          "number": 2,
          "letter": "B",
          "english": "But less than an hour away in the Malaysian countryside, inside three giant, silver domes, scientists are trying to change the future of food. They're pushing the boundaries of what humans eat by growing and processing so-called \"alternative\" crops—such as kedondong. At the headquarters of global research centre Crops For the Future (CFF) this particular under-used fruit has been turned into a sugar-free juice, high in vitamin C and getting top marks in sensory evaluations. \"Anything you see here is a forgotten crop,\" says Sayed Azam-Ali of the abundant plants weaving through the gardens of CFF outside Malaysia's capital Kuala Lumpur.",
          "chinese": "然而，距离这里不到一小时车程的马来西亚乡间，在三座巨大的银色穹顶内，科学家们正努力改变食物的未来。他们通过种植和加工所谓的“替代性”作物，例如酸果，来拓展人类食物的边界。在全球研究中心“未来作物”（Crops For the Future，CFF）的总部，这种一度未被充分利用的水果已经被制成无糖果汁，富含维生素C，并在感官评价中获得了最高评价。“你在这里看到的任何东西，都是一种被遗忘的作物。”在马来西亚首都吉隆坡郊外、CFF花园里生长的各种繁茂植物间，Sayed Azam-Ali 说道。",
