          "number": 8,
          "letter": "H",
          "english": "Undeterred, he ploughed on. Project after project helped prove these crops were viable in different environments as alternatives to the staple ones. But the question of whether these crops would be marketable remained. \"That's the critical thing,\" he says. In one of the centre's domes, food technologist Tan Xinlin uses powdered moringa(辣木) leaves in place of some wheat flour to bake a cake lower in gluten(谷胶) and higher in nutrients. Tan's job is to create recipes with these still-unfamiliar ingredients that will appeal to both local and international tastes. In recent years she has used some of the forgotten crops grown at CFF, such as moringa and bambara groundnut, to make everything from instant soup to Indian snacks. \"I try to modernise forgotten crops instead of using old recipes.\" says Tan, who is also a trained chef. It's a strategy to appeal to the world's growing middle classes who are increasingly turning to the fast and processed food industries. It's also a way to help counter perceptions of local crops as \"old or poor people's food\" or as inferior \"women's crops\", adds Tan.",
          "chinese": "他没有因此却步，而是继续推进。一项又一项的项目帮助证明，这些作物在不同环境中可以作为主食作物的替代品种植。然而，这些作物能否适应市场的问题仍然存在。“这才是关键。”他说。在该中心的一座穹顶内，食品技术专家 Tan Xinlin 用辣木叶粉替代部分小麦粉，烘焙出一种麸质含量更低、营养成分更高的蛋糕。Tan 的工作是利用这些仍不为人熟悉的原料制作食谱，使其同时迎合本地和国际口味。近年来，她一直使用CFF种植的一些被遗忘的作物，例如辣木和班巴拉花生，制作从速食汤到印度小吃的各种食品。“我努力让被遗忘的作物现代化，而不是使用旧食谱。”同样受过厨师训练的 Tan 说道。这种策略旨在吸引世界上不断壮大的中产阶级，他们越来越多地转向快餐和加工食品行业。Tan 补充说，这也是一种帮助消除人们对本地作物的看法的方式，即认为它们是“老年人或穷人的食物”，或是不如其他作物的“女性作物”。",
